amanda: Heads-up: AcmeHealth wants a fast POC. Requirements: multiplexed streaming (single connection per client), function/tool calls in-stream, rigid JSON schema output, EU data residency, and KMS/SSO for Private. They also asked for a 1-week cost ballpark for 500 QPS. Help needed: runbook + legal blurb + tiny SDK snippet we can hand to their engs before tomorrow's demo.

raj: We can support multiplexing over a single websocket using session IDs per stream. Caveats: hosted tier may rebalance; Dedicated with sticky routing is simplest for stable tool-call ordering.

ben: Quick answers: 1) Schema enforcement is app-side; we add "schema_hints" to steering prompts which reduces failures. 2) Tool calls arrive as a structured event; handler must return a JSON string. 3) Ephemeral creds supported via short-lived token service (rotate every 15m by default).

amanda: Can you drop a minimal Node + curl example for the customer? Keep it copy-pasteable.

raj: Sure. Paste this: 

```js
// Node (ws multiplex + tool handler)
const WebSocket = require('ws');
const ws = new WebSocket(process.env.POC_WS_URL);
ws.on('open', () => {
  // open two logical sessions over one ws connection
  ws.send(JSON.stringify({ type: 'open', session_id: 's1', model: 'rwd-stream' }));
  ws.send(JSON.stringify({ type: 'open', session_id: 's2', model: 'rwd-stream' }));
});
ws.on('message', raw => {
  const msg = JSON.parse(raw);
  if (msg.type === 'token') console.log(msg.session_id, msg.token);
  if (msg.type === 'tool_call') {
    // msg = { session_id, name, args }
    // call local adapter and respond
    const result = { ok: true, value: { patient: { id: msg.args.id } } };
    ws.send(JSON.stringify({ type: 'tool_response', session_id: msg.session_id, call_id: msg.call_id, result: JSON.stringify(result) }));
  }
});
```

raj: And a minimal curl handshake example: 

curl -X POST "https://api.redwood.ai/handshake" \n  -H "Authorization: Bearer $RW_KEY" \n  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \n  -d '{"session_id":"s1","schema_hints":{"patient":{"id":"string","name":"string"}}}'

// Response returns {"ws_url":"wss://...","expires_in":900} which you use as POC_WS_URL.

sarah: Legal blurb draft for ephemeral sessions: "Redwood runtime keeps transient KV/cache data in-memory for the lifetime of a session and does not persist raw prompt or response tokens to disk in Dedicated or Private deployments by default. Customers may enable logging; if enabled logs can be exported and will be subject to the agreed retention policy. Customer-managed KMS is used for any at-rest encryption; key rotation follows the customer's KMS policy. Audit records for admin actions and provisioning events are available via the Console and can be retained per contract."

ben: Small ops runbook for the POC engineer (concise):
1) Provide IdP metadata (SAML/OIDC) and KMS ARN.
2) We provision a Dedicated EU test pool and enable ephemeral-role. Expect ~10 business days for full Private on-prem; EU Dedicated test = 2-3 days.
3) We run a handshake endpoint: POST /handshake (auth: bearer) -> returns ws_url + token.
4) Open single websocket, multiplex sessions with session_id field. Send schema_hints on open.
5) On receiving tool_call events, call your adapter webhook and reply with tool_response JSON. Ensure idempotence.
6) After demo: call /rotate-ephemeral to revoke old tokens.

amanda: Pricing ballpark for 500 QPS for 1 week (EU Dedicated test) please? We just need a range to set expectations.

ben: Back-of-envelope: 500 QPS sustained =~ 40-50M tokens/day depending on prompt lengths. For a one-week Dedicated test (reserved pool + on-call support + EU infra) estimate ~$25k-$45k. If they commit to monthly reserved capacity price per token drops by 30-50%. I'll follow up with a formal quote.

raj: One more tech note for the customer: if you need strict ordering + exactly-once tool semantics, prefer Dedicated w/ sticky sessions and make tool handlers idempotent. Hosted is fine for demo but expect possible cross-pod routing under heavy load.

